  {
   "tag": "Why this matters",
   "h": "India's relief explains its climate, rivers, soils and wars",
   "b": "<p>The Himalaya block cold winds and trap the monsoon. The plains feed 1.4 billion people. The plateau holds India's minerals. Almost every Indian geography answer starts with these three. Questions often ask for <b>comparisons</b> and <b>reasons</b>.</p>"
  },
  {
   "tag": "Core idea",
   "h": "Six physiographic divisions",
   "b": "<svg class=\"d\" viewBox=\"0 0 320 204\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"6\" width=\"300\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f1e4c6\" stroke=\"#a8792a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"160\" y=\"23\" fill=\"#7d5a1c\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Northern mountains</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"38\" fill=\"#5d6474\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Himalaya, Purvanchal</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"39\" width=\"300\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dceaec\" stroke=\"#2f6f7a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"160\" y=\"56\" fill=\"#2f6f7a\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Northern plains</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"71\" fill=\"#5d6474\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Indus, Ganga, Brahmaputra alluvium</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"72\" width=\"300\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f4dcd5\" stroke=\"#a3362a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"160\" y=\"89\" fill=\"#a3362a\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Peninsular plateau</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"104\" fill=\"#5d6474\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Oldest, hard rock, Deccan and Central Highlands</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"105\" width=\"300\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f1e4c6\" stroke=\"#a8792a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"160\" y=\"122\" fill=\"#7d5a1c\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Indian desert</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"137\" fill=\"#5d6474\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Thar, west of the Aravalli</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"138\" width=\"300\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dceaec\" stroke=\"#2f6f7a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"160\" y=\"155\" fill=\"#2f6f7a\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Coastal plains</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"170\" fill=\"#5d6474\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Western and Eastern</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"171\" width=\"300\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f3f1ec\" stroke=\"#c9c2b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"160\" y=\"188\" fill=\"#1d2433\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Islands</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"203\" fill=\"#5d6474\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Lakshadweep, Andaman and Nicobar</text></svg><p>Physiography means the study of landforms. India's land area is about 3.28 million sq km. It spans 8 degrees 4 minutes N to 37 degrees 6 minutes N on the mainland.</p>"
  },
  {
   "tag": "Core idea",
   "h": "The Himalaya: three parallel ranges",
   "b": "<svg class=\"d\" viewBox=\"0 0 320 180\"><path d=\"M10 150 L50 150 L80 125 L110 105 L150 50 L190 20 L230 50 L260 75 L310 80 L310 170 L10 170 Z\" fill=\"#f1e4c6\" stroke=\"#a8792a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"22\" y=\"142\" fill=\"#2f6f7a\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"700\">Plains</text><text x=\"75\" y=\"116\" fill=\"#a3362a\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Shiwalik</text><text x=\"112\" y=\"98\" fill=\"#a3362a\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Lesser</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#a3362a\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Greater</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"57\" fill=\"#a3362a\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Himadri</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#7d5a1c\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Tibet</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"16\" fill=\"#5d6474\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">South (plains)  to  North (Tibet)</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"165\" fill=\"#5d6474\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Shiwalik 900-1,200 m, Lesser 3,700-4,500 m, Greater above 6,000 m</text><text x=\"285\" y=\"170\" fill=\"#1d2433\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text></svg><p>They are young <b>fold mountains</b>, about 2,400 km long, arched in a curve from the Indus gorge to the Dihang gorge. Each range has a different rock age and origin.</p>"
  },
  {
   "tag": "Core idea",
   "h": "Himalayan divisions",
   "b": "<p><b>Himadri</b> holds Everest, Kanchenjunga (the highest peak in India, 8,586 m) and the glaciers. <b>Himachal</b>: Pir Panjal, Dhauladhar, Mussoorie, with hill stations and Kashmir valley. <b>Shiwaliks</b>: loose gravel and sandstone, with long valleys called <b>duns</b> (Dehradun) in Uttarakhand and <b>duars</b> in the east.</p><p>By river (Sidney Burrard): <b>Punjab</b> (Indus to Sutlej), <b>Kumaon</b> (Sutlej to Kali), <b>Nepal</b> (Kali to Tista), <b>Assam</b> (Tista to Dihang).</p>"
  },
  {
   "tag": "Core idea",
   "h": "The Northern Plains",
   "b": "<svg class=\"d\" viewBox=\"0 0 320 182\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"6\" width=\"75\" height=\"38\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f1e4c6\" stroke=\"#a8792a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"47.5\" y=\"29.0\" fill=\"#7d5a1c\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Bhabar</text><rect x=\"91\" y=\"6\" width=\"219\" height=\"38\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f3f1ec\" stroke=\"#c9c2b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"200.5\" y=\"29.0\" fill=\"#1d2433\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Pebbly belt at the foothills, streams disappear</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"50\" width=\"75\" height=\"38\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dceaec\" stroke=\"#2f6f7a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"47.5\" y=\"73.0\" fill=\"#2f6f7a\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Terai</text><rect x=\"91\" y=\"50\" width=\"219\" height=\"38\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f3f1ec\" stroke=\"#c9c2b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"200.5\" y=\"73.0\" fill=\"#1d2433\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Marshy, dense forest, reclaimed for farming</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"94\" width=\"75\" height=\"38\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f4dcd5\" stroke=\"#a3362a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"47.5\" y=\"117.0\" fill=\"#a3362a\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Bhangar</text><rect x=\"91\" y=\"94\" width=\"219\" height=\"38\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f3f1ec\" stroke=\"#c9c2b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"200.5\" y=\"117.0\" fill=\"#1d2433\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Old alluvium, kankar nodules, higher</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"138\" width=\"75\" height=\"38\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f3f1ec\" stroke=\"#c9c2b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"47.5\" y=\"161.0\" fill=\"#1d2433\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Khadar</text><rect x=\"91\" y=\"138\" width=\"219\" height=\"38\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f3f1ec\" stroke=\"#c9c2b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"200.5\" y=\"161.0\" fill=\"#1d2433\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">New alluvium, flood plains, very fertile</text></svg><p>Built by alluvium from the Indus, Ganga and Brahmaputra, about 7 lakh sq km. Divisions: Punjab-Haryana plain, Ganga plain, Brahmaputra plain. The plain slopes gently, and rivers form <b>meanders</b>, <b>braided channels</b>, and <b>levees</b>. Frequent floods go with high fertility.</p>"
  },
  {
   "tag": "Core idea",
   "h": "The Peninsular Plateau",
   "b": "<svg class=\"d\" viewBox=\"0 0 320 182\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"6\" width=\"95\" height=\"38\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dceaec\" stroke=\"#2f6f7a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"57.5\" y=\"29.0\" fill=\"#2f6f7a\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Western Ghats</text><rect x=\"111\" y=\"6\" width=\"199\" height=\"38\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f3f1ec\" stroke=\"#c9c2b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"210.5\" y=\"29.0\" fill=\"#1d2433\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Sahyadri, continuous, 1,000-1,500 m, Anamudi 2,695 m</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"50\" width=\"95\" height=\"38\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f1e4c6\" stroke=\"#a8792a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"57.5\" y=\"73.0\" fill=\"#7d5a1c\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Eastern Ghats</text><rect x=\"111\" y=\"50\" width=\"199\" height=\"38\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f3f1ec\" stroke=\"#c9c2b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"210.5\" y=\"73.0\" fill=\"#1d2433\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Broken by rivers, lower, Mahendragiri</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"94\" width=\"95\" height=\"38\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f4dcd5\" stroke=\"#a3362a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"57.5\" y=\"117.0\" fill=\"#a3362a\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Aravalli</text><rect x=\"111\" y=\"94\" width=\"199\" height=\"38\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f3f1ec\" stroke=\"#c9c2b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"210.5\" y=\"117.0\" fill=\"#1d2433\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Oldest fold range, Guru Shikhar 1,722 m</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"138\" width=\"95\" height=\"38\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f3f1ec\" stroke=\"#c9c2b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"57.5\" y=\"161.0\" fill=\"#1d2433\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Deccan Trap</text><rect x=\"111\" y=\"138\" width=\"199\" height=\"38\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f3f1ec\" stroke=\"#c9c2b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"210.5\" y=\"161.0\" fill=\"#1d2433\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Black basalt, Maharashtra plateau</text></svg><p>This is the oldest and most stable block, a part of <b>Gondwanaland</b>. The <b>Narmada-Tapi</b> flow in rift valleys, westwards. The <b>Chotanagpur plateau</b> is the mineral heartland. The <b>Western Ghats</b> are a block mountain edge, and rains to the west are heavy.</p>"
  },
  {
   "tag": "Core idea",
   "h": "Gaps, passes and shape",
   "b": "<p>Western Ghats gaps: <b>Thal</b> (Mumbai-Nashik), <b>Bhor</b> (Mumbai-Pune), <b>Pal</b> (Kerala-Tamil Nadu). Himalayan passes: Shipki La, Nathu La, Zoji La, Banihal, Rohtang, Jelep La. Mountain passes matter for trade and for military moves along the northern border.</p>"
  },
  {
   "tag": "Debate",
   "h": "Developing a fragile mountain",
   "b": "<p>Himalayan roads, tunnels and hydropower bring jobs and security. Yet Joshimath sank in 2023, and the Uttarkashi Silkyara tunnel collapse (November 2023) and the 2013 Kedarnath and 2021 Chamoli floods show the risk. The <b>Char Dham</b> road debate pitted defence needs against ecology; the Supreme Court allowed a wider road in 2021 for the border.</p>"
  },
  {
   "tag": "Indian angle",
   "h": "Why the plains matter, and why the Himalaya shield",
   "b": "<p>The Himalaya turn the monsoon back, so the Gangetic plains get rain, and they shield India from cold Central Asian winds. Glaciers feed the perennial rivers. The plains produce a large share of wheat and rice, and have about 40% of India's people.</p><p>The Western Ghats are a <b>UNESCO World Heritage Site</b> (2012) and a biodiversity hotspot. The Himalaya are a hotspot too.</p>"
  },
  {
   "tag": "Exam lens",
   "cls": "exam",
   "h": "How to write this",
   "b": "<div class=\"box\"><h4>Likely questions</h4><p>\"Describe the physiographic divisions of India and their significance.\" (15 marks)</p><p>\"Himalayan range is vast and complex. Discuss how each range differs.\" (10 marks)</p></div><p><b>Structure:</b> list divisions; a short paragraph with key features and a number each; one line on economic or climatic significance; a current example.</p><p><b>Prelims tip:</b> Kanchenjunga is India's highest peak; Anamudi is the highest peak south of the Himalaya; Narmada flows west through a rift valley.</p>"
  }
